WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.234
-Our next guests
are the co-authors

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:04.036
of the political thriller
"State of Terror,"

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:05.238
which is out now.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.506
One is an award-winning
best-selling author,

00:00:07.506 --> 00:00:10.377
and the other is a former
senator, secretary of state,

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.779
and the winner
of the 2016 popular vote.

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:14.714
Please welcome to the show
Louise Penny

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.949
and Hillary Rodham Clinton.

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:18.751
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:25.591 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:32.465 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:39.305 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:43.142
Welcome to the show!

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:44.543
-Real people!

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:46.412
-Real human beings.
-Real human beings.

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:48.514
-Not bad, right?
Not bad.

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:51.551
Well, we're very -- we're
very lucky to have you both

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.152
on a night
that the audience is here.

00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:55.288
Congratulations on the book.
-Thank you.

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.655
-You've written nonfiction.

00:00:56.655 --> 00:00:58.591
Louise, you have
written mysteries.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.659
This is a thriller.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:00.993
It's a little different
for both of you.

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:02.929
How was your
collaboration process?

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.997
-It was fabulous, I have to say.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:08.234
You know,
Louise and I were friends

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:11.070
before the idea
was presented to us,

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.640
and I really came to know her

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.343
through her amazing series,
the Gamache series,

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.012
that I just loved.

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:21.047
And when the idea was presented,

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.315
I think both of us were,
you know, a little --

00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:25.184
you know, wondering,
how would this work?

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.385
'Cause we were friends.

00:01:26.385 --> 00:01:28.288
We didn't want to undermine
our friendship.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:32.391
And so we dove into it and we
started working on outlines.

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:33.760
-Well, we tried
to figure it out, didn't we?

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.594
Like, what are we
going to write about?

00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:37.564
And, you know, we thought,
first of all,

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:38.864
if you've got
a secretary of state,

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:40.733
maybe you don't want
to write about

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.368
a secretary of transportation.

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.669
-Right.

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:45.604
-So that was clear,
but the rest of it wasn't.

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:47.573
-Did you ever say, "You know,
we could also write

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.275
about a Canadian
mystery author"?

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.911
-Yes, I did!
And Hillary kept nixing it.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.713
-Yeah.
-Classic Hillary.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:56.683
-That was classic Hillary.
Exactly. Yeah, exactly.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:58.885
So then there were times
when we were --

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.686
because this was
in the pandemic, right?

00:02:00.686 --> 00:02:02.188
So we couldn't even
get together.

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:03.656
So we were doing FaceTimes

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.791
and, "What about this
and what about that?"

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:09.195
And then there were times
we got ourselves so confused,

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.064
we thought each other's screen
had frozen

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:13.866
'cause we were just
staring at each other.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:15.634
Like, "Where do we go
from here?"

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:18.171
-We had these dreams
before the pandemic

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.706
that, you know, we'd meet
at a spa somewhere.

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:23.208
-Sure.
-You know, we'd spa all morning.

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:24.877
-We'd spa all morning.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:26.178
-Maybe write for an hour.

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:27.680
We'd go back and spa some more.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.415
-Yes, have a facial, exactly.

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:30.616
-None of that --

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:32.919
-You probably got it done faster
this way.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.455
-I think we probably did.
[ Laughs ]

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:38.858
-So, this is about a new
secretary of state who --

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.193
and there's also a character

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:42.828
who's a former president
who's reckless.

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.330
-You could say that, yeah.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.497
-I think there are other words.

00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:47.600
-He's from Palm Beach.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.134
-Well, a lot of people
live in Palm Beach.

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:50.936
-Yeah, a lot of people
live in -- Right, yeah.

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:54.407
But was he inspired by anyone?
Was there anybody in real life?

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:55.608
-He is fictional, Seth.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:56.642
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-He is fictional.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.911
However, having lived through

00:02:58.911 --> 00:03:01.080
the prior four years...
-Yeah.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.184
-...it wasn't too hard
to imagine.

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:07.820
-Yeah.
-And the president is someone

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.790
who gets the country
into a lot of trouble.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:11.691
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.427
And some of it of his own making

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:16.595
and some of it because
he doesn't understand

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:19.899
how other bad actors
are actually using him.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:24.470
You know, so it wasn't
too difficult for, you know,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.708
Louise and I to think about
this fictional former president.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:30.143
-No.
No, no, no.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.411
But I remember the moment
when I asked you,

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:33.946
like, what -- what
was your nightmare?

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:35.481
When you were
secretary of state,

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:38.284
what kept you awake at night?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:40.753
-And --
-I had a list.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.421
-She had a list.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:44.223
It was terrifying,
so then she went through it,

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:45.391
and I said, "Well, choose one,"

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.493
and you chose
the nuclear option.

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:51.330
-I mean, in the book,
the plot really revolves

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:56.002
around the great fear that
we always had, and still do,

00:03:56.002 --> 00:04:00.673
that terrorists would somehow
get ahold of nuclear weapons,

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.576
either a dirty bomb
or something more sophisticated,

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:06.746
and it's a plot that we hope

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.914
never, ever comes true
in real life.

00:04:08.914 --> 00:04:11.483
But it is something
of a cautionary tale

00:04:11.483 --> 00:04:12.785
about what could happen.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:14.119
-When you were
secretary of state,

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:16.022
I imagine briefings
all the time,

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:19.125
and you would have a great
burden, I would imagine, on you

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.993
to make sure you were
up to speed on everything.

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:23.162
But it would also,
I would imagine, be important

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:25.831
to find time to, like, shut
your brain down a little bit.

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:27.233
-Right.
-Did you in those times --

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.835
Were you reading fiction
about worst-case scenarios,

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:31.938
or were you reading
even lighter stuff?

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:34.540
-Lighter stuff, lighter stuff,
but also classic mysteries,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.176
like the ones
that Louise writes.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:41.080
You know, and "People"
and "Us Weekly."

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.282
I mean, I was trying to keep up

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:44.750
with what was actually
going on in the world,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.452
not just what was
in my briefings.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.921
-Whereas I was reading
intelligence reports.

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.389
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:52.892
-I have a lot more questions
for both of you.

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:55.427
We will be right back with more
from Hillary Rodham Clinton

00:04:55.427 --> 00:04:56.429
and Louise Penny.

